<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nueva Planta decrees</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1716">1716</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nueva Planta decrees were a series of laws issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> between 1707 and 1716, during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. Punishing the territories of the Crown of Aragon that had backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> claimant, they abolished the separate laws, parliaments and institutions of Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> and Catalonia and imposed the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, creating a more unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees.jpg" alt="Nueva Planta decrees" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ordenada pel Rei d'Espanya Felip V. Impresa per Joseph Teixidó. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nova_planta_Catalunya_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The decrees were signed between 1707 and 1716 by Philip V, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, during and shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, which ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a>. Philip was angered by what he saw as sedition by the realms of the Crown of Aragon, which had supported the claim of Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to the Spanish thrones, and he took his native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as his model of a centralised state. He suppressed the institutions, privileges and ancient charters (fueros in Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> in Catalan) of almost all the territories of the Crown of Aragon.</p><p>The Basque territories and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, which had at first supported Philip, kept their home rule after he backed down from his intent to suppress it following a military campaign against a Basque uprising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The decrees ruled that all the territories of the Crown of Aragon except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-daran">Aran Valley</a> were to be governed by the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, described in the 1707 decree as the most praiseworthy in all the universe. The acts were promulgated in 1707 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and Aragon and in 1715 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>. The decree for Catalonia was issued on 9 October 1715 and sent out from 16 January 1716.</p><p>They ended the Courts of Aragon, Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, whose members were summoned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-castile">Cortes of Castile</a>, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Internal borders and tariffs were erased, except in the Basque territory, and all subjects gained the right to trade with the American and Asian colonies. Top civil servants were appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, and Castilian became the sole language of government, displacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Catalan and others, with one exception: Basque could be used in the Vizcaya Chamber of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In Catalonia the decree abolished the Generalitat, the General Court, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-cent">Consell de Cent</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and most royal officers. A Captain General replaced the viceroy, the principality was divided into twelve corregimientos on the Castilian model, and the sometents, popular militias, were banned. A cadastre taxed property, labour, commerce and industry, multiplying the tax burden sevenfold in a land already devastated by the war.</p><p>The six Catalan universities were closed and replaced by a royal university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cervera">Cervera</a>. Catalan private law survived, and was later codified in the Civil Code of Catalonia, but its criminal and mercantile law eroded through royal ordinances during the 18th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/val-daran">Aran Valley</a> and the Valleys of Andorra escaped the decree. Secret instructions told new officials to introduce Castilian by temperate and disguised measures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nueva Planta decrees” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Almansa, Philip V abolished the laws of Aragon and Valencia by the <b>Nueva Planta decrees</b> of 1707, though Aragonese civil law was partly restored in 1711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Because Aragon, Valencia and Catalonia had largely backed the Habsburg claimant, Philip abolished their separate institutions by the <b>Nueva Planta decrees</b> of 1707–1716 and imposed Castilian law and administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…claimant Archduke Charles against the Bourbon Philip V. After the Bourbon victory at the Battle of Almansa on 25 April 1707, Philip V abolished the fueros of Aragon and Valencia by right of conquest in a decree of 29 June 1707, the first of the <b>Nueva Planta decrees</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>These institutions survived until the <b>Nueva Planta</b> decree of 1716.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The War of the Spanish Succession confirmed him as king but cost Spain its European possessions at Utrecht in 1713, while the <b>Nueva Planta decrees</b> abolished the separate institutions of the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-spain">Bourbon Spain</a></li><li><blockquote>Majorca remained a separate realm within the Crown until the <b>Nueva Planta</b> decree of 1715.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
